Cisco Modeling Labs (CML) — VMware Installation Guide

Create a Cisco Account

Visit Cisco.

Select Log In → Create Account.

Complete registration and verify your email address.

Sign in using your Cisco account.

A Cisco account is used to access software downloads and manage CML licensing.

Obtain a CML License
Visit the Cisco Learning Network Store.

Choose the appropriate Cisco Modeling Labs offering, such as CML Personal, or use an eligible CML Free installation.

For this project, a personal CML license with a five-node limit was used.

After obtaining the license, access the associated downloads using the Cisco account.

Download Cisco Modeling Labs
Download the following files from Cisco’s software portal:

File Purpose
CML Controller .ova Virtual machine containing the CML server and web interface
Reference Platform .iso Cisco virtual router and switch images

Both files are required for a standard VMware-based installation.

Official download portal: Cisco Software Central

Download and Install VMware Workstation
Visit the VMware Workstation product page.

Follow the VMware/Broadcom download instructions.
Download a supported VMware Workstation version for Windows.
Run the installer.
Complete installation.
Restart Windows if requested.
VMware Workstation provides the virtualization environment that hosts the CML controller.

Enable Hardware Virtualization
Before running CML, verify that hardware virtualization is enabled.

On Windows:

Open Task Manager.
Select Performance → CPU.
Check that Virtualization: Enabled appears.
If disabled, enable Intel VT-x or AMD-V/SVM in the computer’s BIOS/UEFI.

CML requires nested virtualization because its virtual routers run inside the CML controller VM.

Import CML into VMware

Open VMware Workstation.

Select File → Open.

Locate the downloaded CML .ova file.

Import the virtual machine.

Choose a storage location.

Wait for the import to finish.

Do not power on the VM yet. Cisco requires reviewing the VM configuration first.

Configure the CML Virtual Machine
Open Edit Virtual Machine Settings.

Configure the resources according to your computer’s available capacity and Cisco’s requirements.

Resource Example allocation
Processors 4 or more vCPUs
RAM 8–16 GB
Storage 32 GB or more
Network NAT or bridged, depending on design
Virtualization Nested virtualization enabled

Attach the downloaded Reference Platform ISO to the VM’s virtual CD/DVD drive.

For a local standalone installation, NAT networking is often sufficient. Bridged networking may be more useful when the CML server must be directly reachable from other machines on the same network.

The correct network mode depends on the intended topology.

Start CML and Complete Initial Setup

Power on the CML VM.

Open its VMware console.

Follow the initial configuration wizard.

Configure the CML administrator account.

Configure networking using DHCP or an appropriate static configuration.

Complete the remaining setup prompts.

The wizard configures the CML server before its web interface becomes available.

